    "experiments": [
        {
            "number": 3,
            "title": "CMOS D flip flop design and analysis",
            "date": "27/08/2026",
            "sections": [
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "AIM",
                    "text": "To design and verify dynamic D flip-flops in Cadence Virtuoso using a master-slave configuration with (1) using transmission-gate (2) using C²MOS and to calculate the setup time, clock-to-Q delay, and maximum operating frequency for 1–1 and 0–0 overlapping conditions."
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "TOOL",
                    "text": "Cadence Virtuoso"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "THEORY",
                    "text": [
                        "A dynamic D flip-flop stores the input data and transfers it to the output according to the clock transition. In a master-slave configuration, two storage stages operate using complementary clock phases. The master stage captures the input data, while the slave stage transfers the stored data to the output, providing edge-triggered operation.",
                        "In the transmission-gate-based implementation, a D latch is first designed using a CMOS transmission gate. Two such latches are then connected in a master-slave configuration with complementary clock signals to form the D flip-flop. In the C²MOS-based implementation, the master-slave D flip-flop is directly designed using clock-controlled CMOS stages.",
                        "The setup time ($t_{setup}$) is the minimum time for which the input D must remain stable before the active clock edge for correct data capture. The clock-to-Q delay ($t_{C-Q}$) is the time interval between the active clock edge and the corresponding transition at the output Q.",
                        "For reliable operation, the minimum clock period is given by $T_{min}=t_{setup}+t_{C-Q}$. Hence, the maximum operating frequency is $f_{max}=1/T_{min}$. These timing parameters are determined for both 1–1 and 0–0 overlapping conditions using transient simulation in Cadence Virtuoso.",
                        "The designed D flip-flops are simulated using 180 nm CMOS technology, and the obtained waveforms are used to calculate the setup time, clock-to-Q delay, minimum clock period, and maximum operating frequency."
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "table",
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Tables",
                    "tables": [
                        {
                            "title": "Design Parameters",
                            "csv": "param.csv"
                        },
                        {
                            "title": "Result comparison (pre layout vs post layout) of Transmission Gate based dff",
                            "csv": "tx.csv"
                        },
                        {
                            "title": "Result comparison (pre layout vs post layout) of C2MOS based dff",
                            "csv": "c2mos.csv"
                        }
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "CALCULATION for transmission gate based d flip flop",
                    "new_page": false,
                    "text": [
                        "$t_{setup(pre)}=(80.0015-79.95976)*1000=41.74ps$",
                        "$t_{setup(post)}=(80.0015-79.92831)*1000=73.19ps$",
                        "$t_{C-Q(pre)}=(80.41886-80.0015)*1000=417.36ps$",
                        "$t_{C-Q(post)}=(80.86858-80.0015)*1000=867.08ps$",
                        "$T_{min(pre)}=t_{setup(pre)}+t_{C-Q(pre)}=41.74+417.36=459.10ps$",
                        "$T_{min(post)}=t_{setup(post)}+t_{C-Q(post)}=73.19+867.08=940.27ps$",
                        "$f_{max(pre)}=1/T_{min(pre)}=1/459.10=2.178GHz$",
                        "$f_{max(post)}=1/T_{min(post)}=1/940.27=1.063GHz$"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "CALCULATION for c2mos based d flip flop",
                    "new_page": false,
                    "text": [
                        "$t_{setup(pre)}=(10.0025-9.961437)*1000=41.063ps$",
                        "$t_{setup(post)}=(10.0025-9.961437)*1000=41.063ps$",
                        "$t_{C-Q(pre)}=(10.09786-10.0025)*1000=95.36ps$",
                        "$t_{C-Q(post)}=(10.15533-10.0025)*1000=152.83ps$",
                        "$T_{min(pre)}=t_{setup(pre)}+t_{C-Q(pre)}=41.063+95.36=136.423ps$",
                        "$T_{min(post)}=t_{setup(post)}+t_{C-Q(post)}=41.063+152.83=193.893ps$",
                        "$f_{max(pre)}=1/T_{min(pre)}=1/136.423=7.330GHz$",
                        "$f_{max(post)}=1/T_{min(post)}=1/193.893=5.157GHz$"
                    ]
                },
